      array['Limit each slide to a single message', 'Explain the redundancy effect and why reading your slides aloud fails', 'Use size and contrast to direct the audience''s eye'],
      'The most useful twenty minutes in this level. Five rules, each with a reason behind it rather than taste.

**One message per slide.** If a slide makes two points, it is two slides. **Don''t put your script on the slide and then read it** — this is the redundancy effect, and it actively harms comprehension because people cannot read and listen at the same time. Your audience will read ahead, finish before you, and stop listening. **Make the most important element the biggest**, because size is how the eye decides what to look at first. **Use contrast** to point at what matters. **Never more than about six objects on a slide**, which is roughly the limit of what working memory holds at once.

