    (v_m2, 'Handling Nerves', 'Why stage fright happens physically, and the countermeasures that actually work.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K93fMnFKwfI', 3,
      array['Explain the physical mechanism behind stage fright', 'Apply rehearsal and breathing techniques before presenting', 'Reframe nerves as a normal physical response rather than a personal failing'],
      'Four minutes, and worth it for the reframe alone. Stage fright is your fight-or-flight system reading an audience as a threat and flooding you with adrenaline. That is a physiological event, not evidence that you are bad at this — which matters, because most people interpret the racing heart as proof they will fail and then get more nervous.

The countermeasures are unglamorous and effective: **rehearse until the material is automatic**, so the part of your brain that panics isn''t also trying to remember what comes next. **Practise in the actual room** if you can. Exercise beforehand burns off some adrenaline; slow deep breathing tells your nervous system the threat is not real. And visualising it going well is not wishful thinking — it is rehearsal your body responds to.

Rehearsal is the one that does the most work. Nearly every presenter who seems calm has simply run it more times than you''d guess.',
      'Rehearse your opening two minutes out loud, standing, five times. Notice how much less you need the notes by the fifth. Then try slow breathing — four counts in, six out — for a minute beforehand.',
